From deb6a250332f3b2eb08085767f86fd8dd02f1958 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: cohost u3 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Mon, 2 Nov 2026 20:49:55 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] 30: the inline (NaN-boxed) integer holds 48 bits, not 32

Draft: doc/upstream/jank-30-inline-int-width.md in johnmn3/cohost.cljc. Applies after
the series up to 27 (12-21's i64 arithmetic is not edited). Repro: bash
dev/research/upstream/run.sh 30-inline-int-width.

With 12-21, a sum is correct past 2^31, and every add past it allocated an
obj::integer (make_box tagged only the i32 range): reduce + over a 1e6 vector went
7.5 ms -> 35-47 ms. The integer tag 0xFFF9 leaves the low 48 bits free (the pointer tag
uses 48 too), so:

- oref.hpp: integer_value_mask is 48 bits; min/max_small_integer are -2^47..2^47-1;
  as_integer sign-extends the payload into an i64; tag(i64).
- obj::small_integer and oref<obj::small_integer> hold an i64 (the 48-bit value).
- make_box(integral) tags anything in 48 bits, heap-allocates only beyond.
- math.hpp, the boxed add/sub/mul arms (12-21's): the fast path's width check moves to
  the inline bound: add and sub of two operands within 47 bits run in i64 (no overflow
  possible) and tag; mul of two i32 operands runs in i64; everything else takes 12-21's
  checked i64 path unchanged (overflow still throws), which now tags a result in 48 bits.
- codegen (cpp_processor.cpp, api.{hpp,cpp}) and the analyzer's literal typing
  (cpp_util.cpp): a boxed literal is a small_integer up to 48 bits; _jank_small_int
  takes an i64.

Files: include/cpp/jank/runtime/oref.hpp, include/cpp/jank/runtime/obj/number.hpp,
src/cpp/jank/runtime/obj/number.cpp, include/cpp/jank/runtime/core/make_box.hpp,
include/cpp/jank/runtime/core/math.hpp, src/cpp/jank/codegen/cpp_processor.cpp,
src/cpp/jank/codegen/api.cpp, include/cpp/jank/codegen/api.hpp, src/cpp/jank/analyze/cpp_util.cpp.
---
